<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odaenathus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 220–267</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odaenathus was a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>. After the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> captured the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> in 260, he attacked the retreating Persian army, suppressed the usurpers Macrianus and Quietus, and raided Sasanian territory as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> recognised him as corrector of the whole East. He was assassinated around 267, and Zenobia then ruled in the name of their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odaenathus.jpg" alt="Odaenathus" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harald Ingholt, Henri Seyrig and Jean Starcky, followed by linguistic remarks by André Caquot. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tessera_from_Palmyra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Septimius Odaenathus was born around 220 into an aristocratic Palmyrene family that had received Roman citizenship in the 190s under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severan-dynasty">Severan dynasty</a>. He was the son of Hairan, and a tomb inscription gives his descent from Wahb Allat and Nasor. Palmyra was an autonomous city in the province of Syria Phoenice, ruled by a council with no tradition of hereditary monarchy. After the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in 224 disrupted its trade, the council probably elected a lord, called ras, to lead a stronger army. Odaenathus held that office, created for him, as early as the 240s, and by 258 he bore the Roman rank of consularis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> defeated and captured the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> near Edessa in 260. According to the Augustan History, Odaenathus was declared king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> as soon as the news reached the city. He fell on the retreating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> and Zeugma in late summer 260. When the usurper Fulvius Macrianus took his sons as joint emperors against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a>, Odaenathus waited until it was clear that Gallienus would win, then marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, where Quietus was killed and Balista executed in autumn 261.</p><p>From 262 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, then reached the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, which withstood a short siege. About 263 he took the title King of Kings of the East and crowned his son Herodianus as co-king. By then he effectively controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, Roman Mesopotamia and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and he ruled in practice as an independent monarch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was assassinated together with Herodianus in late 267, near Heraclea Pontica or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>. The sources disagree on the killer: a namesake, an unnamed nephew or a cousin named Maeonius. John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> accused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a>, though the evidence for a Roman conspiracy is weak. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> succeeded him under the regency of his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>, who used the power he had built to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a> in 270. He is regarded as the founder of the Palmyrene royal dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odaenathus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Persian king Shapur I captured the emperor Valerian in 260, the Palmyrene noble <b>Odaenathus</b> defeated the retreating Persians and crushed the usurpers in the East, earning from Gallienus the title corrector of the whole East.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in Heraclea Pontica, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.56,38.27&amp;year=250" aria-label="Palmyra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Odaenathus"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5580 4589h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5580 4589h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.56,38.27&amp;year=250">What was here in 250 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
